## etcd: new client library

The etcd execution module, state module, `etcd3` cache and
`salt.utils.etcd_util` now use [etcd3gw](https://pypi.org/project/etcd3gw/)
instead of `etcd3-py`, which has not had a release since 2019. Configuration
is unchanged, including `etcd.username`/`etcd.password` and the TLS options.
etcd3gw needs Python 3.10 or later.

To upgrade, install the new library on each master and minion that talks to
etcd. Salt no longer uses `etcd3-py`, so it can be removed.

```bash
salt-pip install etcd3gw
```

This release also changes some etcd behavior:

- Recursive operations (`etcd.rm ... recurse=True`, `etcd.tree`, `etcd.ls`,
  `etcd.get ... recurse=True` and recursive watches) used a plain prefix, so
  `/app` also matched `/application` and `/app-old`; a recursive delete of
  `/app` deleted those keys too. They now cover only the key itself and the
  keys under `/app/`.
- Errors talking to etcd (unreachable server, bad credentials, permission
  denied) used to return `None`, the same as a missing key. They now raise,
  so `etcd.get` and the other functions fail instead of returning nothing.
  `None` still means the key does not exist.
- `etcd.watch` with `timeout=0`, the default, returned `None` at once. It
  now waits until the key changes.
- `etcd.username` without `etcd.password`, or only one of `etcd.client_cert`
  and `etcd.client_key`, raises an error instead of being ignored. A client
  certificate without `etcd.ca` now uses HTTPS with the system CA bundle
  instead of plain HTTP.
- The `etcd.set`, `etcd.rm` and `etcd.directory` states honor `test=True`.
  `etcd.set` no longer rewrites a key that already has the right value, can
  create a key when no profile is given, and fails the state when etcd
  returns an error. `etcd.wait_rm` keeps `recurse` when triggered.
  `etcd.directory` fails with an explanation, since etcd v3 has no
  directories.

